<%# The admin shell for the owner, on the <wa-page> of Web Awesome.
    ⚠️ The name is NOT `application`, on purpose. That is the default layout of Rails, and this app
    is for machines by default: each widget controller and each API controller renders with
    `layout false`. The name `admin` keeps them the same if a person removes one of those
    declarations. %>
<%# `wa-brand-red` changes the brand colors of Web Awesome from its default blue to the red
    family. styles/_tokens.scss then sets the loud step to the exact firebrick of the site. The
    dark-mode script changes only `wa-dark`, thus it never changes this class. %>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" class="wa-brand-red">
  <head>
    <%= render "layouts/head", title: yield(:title) %>
  </head>

  <body>
    <%# `disable-navigation-toggle` is here because the server renders this page. The component
        sets it by itself after it sees our [data-toggle-nav], but only after the browser upgrades
        the component. Until then, its own menu button renders on a row with no styles, above the
        header bar. `wa-cloak` hides the shell until each custom element has a definition. %>
    <wa-page class="admin wa-cloak" disable-navigation-toggle mobile-breakpoint="768px">
      <header slot="header" class="admin-header">
        <%# ⚠️ The name is a visually-hidden span, and not an `aria-label`. <wa-button> renders its
            own <button> in a shadow root and it sends no ARIA of the host to that button, and
            `icon_svg` marks each SVG `aria-hidden`. Thus an aria-label here would leave the button
            with no name. %>
        <%# ⚠️ Do not add `wa-mobile-only`. `wa-base` already hides `[data-toggle-nav]` on the
            desktop, and it does that *without* `!important`. That is what lets _page.scss show the
            button on the first frame. The class adds an `!important` that no unlayered rule can
            win against, and the wordmark then moves to the side at the load. %>
        <wa-button data-toggle-nav appearance="plain">
          <%= raw icon_svg("classic", "light", "bars") %>
          <span class="wa-visually-hidden"><%= t("admin.layout.open_menu") %></span>
        </wa-button>
        <%= link_to root_path, class: "admin-header__home", aria: { label: t("admin.layout.home") } do %>
          <%= render "layouts/logo" %>
        <% end %>
      </header>

      <%# This markup is here one time. <wa-page> renders it as the desktop sidebar and moves the
          same copy into a drawer on a mobile screen. It never shows both at the same time, thus do
          not put a copy in the header. %>
      <nav slot="navigation" class="admin-nav" aria-label="<%= t("admin.layout.nav_label") %>">
        <%= render "layouts/admin_nav" %>
      </nav>

      <div slot="navigation-footer" class="admin-nav__footer">
        <%# This uses form_with with a <wa-button type="submit">, and not button_to, which writes a
            native <button>. The button of Web Awesome is part of its form, thus it submits that
            form as a native button does. %>
        <%= form_with url: "/signout", method: :post do %>
          <wa-button type="submit" appearance="outlined" variant="neutral">
            <%= slotted_icon_svg("classic", "light", "arrow-right-from-bracket") %>
            <%= t("admin.layout.sign_out") %>
          </wa-button>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <main class="admin-main">
        <%= yield %>
      </main>
    </wa-page>

    <%# The Republish dialog, which the nav item of the same name opens. It is outside <wa-page>, on
        purpose: refer to the comment at the top of the partial. %>
    <%= render "layouts/republish_dialog" %>

    <%# The toast stack. It is a sibling of <wa-page> for the same reason as the dialog, and the
        layout renders it one time, thus each page and the Republish dialog write into this stack.
        app/javascript/lib/toast.js finds it by this id.
        ⚠️ `wa-cloak` hides it until <wa-toast-item> has a definition. Without it, the words of a
        flash show as plain text at the end of the page for the time of that upgrade. %>
    <wa-toast id="notifications" class="wa-cloak" placement="bottom-end" data-controller="toast">
      <%# The flash of a redirect. <wa-toast> starts the timer of each item that its slot receives,
          thus an item that the server renders shows itself and needs no code of ours. %>
      <% flash.each do |type, message| %>
        <wa-toast-item variant="<%= type.to_s == "alert" ? "danger" : "success" %>"><%= message %></wa-toast-item>
      <% end %>
    </wa-toast>
  </body>
</html>
